      "knowledge_point": "Applying De Moivre's Theorem With a Negative Sine",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression ${[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}^{16}.$",
      "worked_solution": "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=16$ and $\\phi =\\frac{\\theta }{2}.$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\theta }{2})]}^{16} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(16\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{2})-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(16\\cdot \\frac{\\theta }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
