      "math_academy_id": "q-325325",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4ed5facf-f654-5890-974f-bbdcd9641515",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying De Moivre's Theorem to Fractions",
      "problem": "Simplify the expression $\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}},$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}}=3{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-4}.\n$$\n\nDe Moivre's Theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-4$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{4}} & =3{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )]}^{-4} \\\\  & =3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-4\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-4\\theta )]=3[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )].\n$$",
