    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-325346",
      "knowledge_point_id": "cbc91582-8d44-5de1-9c98-1c5de173eefb",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying De Moivre's Theorem With a Negative Sine",
      "problem": "Express $\\frac{4}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]^{5}}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$\\frac{4}{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]^{5}}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}^{5}} & =4{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}^{-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=-5$ and $\\phi =4\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}^{-5} & =4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-5\\cdot 4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-5\\cdot 4\\theta )] \\\\  & =4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-20\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-20\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\phi )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\phi )$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\phi )=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\phi )$ for any input $\\phi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-20\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-20\\theta )] & =4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )-[-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta )]] \\\\  & =4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta )].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n4{[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta )]}^{-5}=4[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(20\\theta )+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(20\\theta )].\n$$",
